Barmah Forest Virus Polyarthralgia
Complex Fatigue Entities
Post-viral inflammatory joint pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-004
Reason for Visit: Urgent assessment of severe, migrating joint pain and extreme distress preventing sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of excruciating, shifting polyarthralgia and fever history. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to rule out septic arthritis and acute systemic infection, and to provide potent after-hours analgesia to halt severe distress.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My joints ache so deeply I can't sleep, and it moves from my knees to my wrists.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 5-day history of extreme fatigue and an earlier maculopapular rash history that has since faded. Over the last 48 hours, they developed persistent shifting joint pain. The pain began in the right knee, migrated to the left ankle, and is tonight severely affecting both wrists. Patient is exhausted due to complete lack of sleep from the constant throbbing.
Functional Status: Independent but severely limited tonight due to upper limb joint pain preventing weight-bearing or lifting.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to open medication bottles or prepare food due to wrist synovitis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/75 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Fatigued, tearful due to pain, frequently repositioning limbs.
Physical Exam: Mild low-grade temperature. Examination of joints reveals non-erythematous, mildly effused bilateral wrists and left ankle. Pain exquisitely worse on active range of motion. No gross joint deformity. Presentation is archetypal for post-viral inflammatory joint pain.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment. Patient had difficulty unlocking the front door due to wrist pain.
Medication Reconciliation: Using over-the-counter paracetamol with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Barmah Forest Virus Polyarthralgia - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute septic arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Polyarticular migrating nature rather than monoarticular, and absence of severe localized erythema or high-grade fever.

Rheumatoid arthritis acute onset - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear antecedent viral prodrome with maculopapular rash, though serology will ultimately be required to differentiate.

Ross River Virus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clinically indistinguishable without serology; managed identically in the acute phase.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe polyarthralgia causing significant functional impairment and sleep deprivation. The urgency of the home visit was justified by the need to clinically exclude acute septic arthritis—a joint-destroying medical emergency—and to provide prescription-grade anti-inflammatory management. Delaying care would have resulted in unmanageable pain, profound psychological distress, and potential functional crisis due to the patient's inability to care for themselves overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Meloxicam 15mg daily for potent anti-inflammatory coverage. Targeted intervention strategy includes COX-2 inhibitors, PEA, and Omega-3. Ordered Barmah Forest and Ross River virus serology to be completed via routine pathology tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your anti-inflammatory medication strictly with your morning meal, you will establish a consistent blood level of the drug, effectively suppressing the migrating joint pain and rewarding you with restored sleep.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Electric jar/can openers recommended until wrist synovitis resolves.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 7 days to review viral serology results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to ED if any single joint becomes bright red, hot to touch, dramatically swollen, or if fever spikes above 39.0 C.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending specific viral serology; post-viral syndromes can fluctuate.
Clinical Course: Viral polyarthralgia can be protracted, lasting weeks to months, but is not jointly destructive. Symptoms will wax and wane but should gradually improve with targeted NSAID therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the Emergency Department if one specific joint suddenly becomes fiery red and hot, or if you develop uncontrollable shivering.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The specific viral infection has left behind inflammatory proteins in your joints; the prescribed medication directly blocks these proteins, turning off the pain signal.
Actionable Steps: Rest the acutely painful joints tonight but engage in gentle range-of-motion exercises tomorrow; keeping the joints moving prevents secondary stiffness, rewarding you with faster functional recovery.

